    { h: 'Lahbab, the red dunes', html: `<p>This is where the safari industry operates. Forty five minutes to an hour out on the E66, dunes to around sixty metres, and sand that carries enough iron oxide to read orange to red in low light. The camps sit inside the dune field, so you drive on sand for the last stretch rather than parking at the edge.</p><p>It is the right destination for a safari because it does two things at once: the dunes are big enough for the drive to be worth doing, and the area is broad enough that a camp can sit in it without the whole trip feeling like a car park. Every package on the <a href="${SAFARI_BASE}">safari page</a> ends up here.</p>` },
    { h: 'Big Red, further along the same road', html: `<p>Big Red is a single very large dune closer to Hatta, and it is the one most visitors have heard of. It has a roadside strip of rental outfits and it is busy, loud and popular with quad riders rather than safari vehicles.</p><p>If somebody has described a desert safari to you that involved queueing at the roadside, they were probably at Big Red rather than at a camp. We run buggy and quad pickup along that stretch, and the <a href="/locations/dune-buggy-big-red-hatta-road/">Big Red page</a> covers it, but the safaris do not stop there.</p>` },
    { h: 'Fossil Rock, and why it is different', html: `<p>Fossil Rock sits towards Sharjah and the sand there is firmer, with a rock outcrop at the centre of it. It is a better ride than it is a photograph, which is why it appears on buggy and dirt bike routes far more often than it appears on safari itineraries.</p><p>Riders who have done Lahbab often ask for it second, because harder sand behaves differently under a machine. The <a href="/locations/dune-buggy-fossil-rock/">Fossil Rock page</a> has the drive times and what the terrain asks of you.</p>` },
    { h: 'Getting the light right matters more than the operator', html: `<p>The single biggest variable in how the red dunes look is not which company you book, it is what time you arrive. In the hour after sunrise and the hour before sunset the colour is genuinely deep. At midday the same sand looks beige.</p><p>That is why safaris run at the two ends of the day and why the <a href="${SAFARI_BASE}morning-desert-safari/">morning safari</a> is the one photographers book. It is also why summer visitors should take the morning slot: from June to September the afternoon out here is uncomfortable enough to spoil the trip, and the sand looks identical either way.</p>` }
